\section{Introduction}

In \cite{BH} the authors proved that a closed convex subset $C$ of a complete linear metric space $X$ is polyhedral in its linear hull if and only if $C$ hides no infinite subset $A\subset X\setminus C$ in the sense that $[a,b]\cap C\ne\emptyset$ for any distinct points $a,b\in A$. In this paper we shall prove a similar ``hidden'' characterization of approximatively polyhedral subsets in Banach spaces, simultaneously giving a characterization of separable components of the space of closed convex subsets $\Conv_{\mathsf H}(X)$ of a Banach space $X$.


For a Banach space $X$ by $\Conv_{\mathsf H}(X)$ we denote the space of all non-empty closed convex subsets of $X$,  endowed with the {\em Hausdorff metric}
$$\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(A,B)=\max\{\sup_{a\in A}\mathsf{dist}(a,B),\sup_{b\in B}\mathsf{dist}(b,A)\}\in[0,\infty].$$
Here $\mathsf{dist}(a,B)=\inf\limits_{b\in B}\|a-b\|$ stands for the distance from a point $a\in X$ to a subset $B\subset X$ of the Banach space $X$.

It is well-known  that for each closed convex set $C\in\Conv_{\mathsf H}(X)$ the Hausdorff distance $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt$ restricted to the set
$$\mathcal H_C=\{A\in\Conv_{\mathsf H}(X):\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(A,C)<\infty\}$$is a metric, see \cite[Ch2]{Sendov}. The obtained metric space $(\mathcal H_C,\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt)$ will be called the {\em Hausdorff metric component} (or just {\em component}) of the set $C$ in $\Conv_{\mathsf H}(X)$.

In fact, the present investigation was motivated by the problem of calculating the density of components of the space $\Conv_{\mathsf H}(X)$ and detecting closed convex subsets $C\subset X$ with separable component $\mathcal H_C$. In this paper we shall characterize such sets $C$ in terms of approximative polyhedrality as well as in ``hidden'' terms resembling those from \cite{BH}.

A convex subset $C$ of a Banach space $X$ is called
\begin{itemize}
\item {\em a closed half-space} if $C=f^{-1}([a,+\infty)$ for  some non-zero linear continuous functional $f:X\to\mathbb R$ and some real number $a\in\mathbb R$;
\item {\em polyhedral} if $C$ can be written as the intersection $C=\cap \mathcal F$ of a finite family $\mathcal F$ of closed half-spaces in $X$;
\item {\em approximatively polyhedral} if for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a closed polyhedral subset $P\subset X$ that lies on  Hausdorff distance $\mathsf{d}_{\mathsf H}\kern-1pt(C,P)<\varepsilon$ from $C$.
\end{itemize}

